o{$><$}mn{$>$}2{$<$}/mn{$><$}mi{$>\pi<$}/mi{$><$}mspace width="0.2em"{$><$}/mspace{$><$}msubsup{$><$}mi{$>$}Re{$<$}/mi{$><$}mi{$>\tau<$}/mi{$><$}mi{$>$}n{$<$}/mi{$><$}/msubsup{$><$}mo{$>$}){$<$}/mo{$><$}/mrow{$><$}/math{$>$}, where St is Stuart number and T+T+{$<$}math display="inline" overflow="scroll" altimg="eq-00005.gif"{$><$}mrow{$><$}msup{$><$}mi{$>$}T{$<$}/mi{$><$}mo{$>$}+{$<$}/mo{$><$}/msup{$><$}/mrow{$><$}/math{$>$} is pulsing time scale in wall-layer variables, approximately in the same manner as two-dimensional actuators do. However, the exponent nn{$<$}math display="inline" overflow="scroll" altimg="eq-00006.gif"{$><$}mrow{$><$}mi{$>$}n{$<$}/mi{$><$}/mrow{$><$}/math{$>$} is zero---not 1.0 like that in two-dimensional actuators. At Re{$\tau\gg$}103Re{$\tau\gg$}103{$<$}math display="inline" overflow="scroll" altimg="eq-00007.gif"{$><$}mrow{$><$}msub{$><$}mi{$>$}Re{$<$}/mi{$><$}mi{$>\tau<$}/mi{$><$}/msub{$><$}mo{$>\gg<$}/mo{$><$}msup{$><$}mn{$>$}10{$<$}/mn{$><$}mn{$>$}3{$<$}/mn{$><$}/msup{$><$}/mrow{$><$}/math{$>$}, the same three-dimensional actuators would no longer match the footprints of the unit turbulence production domains and the same applied power would be a weaker perturbation on the streak vorticity. A denser clustering of three-dimensional actuators for the same input power is a likely solution for higher Reynolds numbers.},
