  abstract = {The rotational kinematics of inertial cylinders in homogeneous isotropic turbulence is investigated via laboratory experiments. The effects of particle size and shape on rotation statistics are measured for near-neutrally buoyant particles whose sizes are within the inertial subrange of turbulence. To examine the effects of particle size, three right-circular cylinders (aspect ratio {$\lambda$}=1{$\lambda$}=1\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D706\textbraceright =1) are considered, with size deq=16{$Hdeq$}=16{$H$}d\_\textbraceleft eq\textbraceright =16\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D702\textbraceright, 27{$H$}27{$H$}27\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D702\textbraceright{} and 67{$H$}67{$H$}67\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D702\textbraceright. Here, deq{$deq$}d\_\textbraceleft eq\textbraceright{} is the diameter of a sphere whose volume is equal to that of the particle and {$HH\backslash$}unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D702\textbraceright{} is the Kolmogorov length scale. Results show that the variance of the particle rotation rate follows a -4/3-4/3-4/3 power-law scaling with respect to deq{$deq$}d\_\textbraceleft eq\textbraceright. To examine the effect of particle shape, two cylinders with identical volumes and different aspect ratios ({$\lambda$}=1{$\lambda$}=1\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D706\textbraceright =1 and {$\lambda$}=4{$\lambda$}=4\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D706\textbraceright =4) are measured. Their motion also scales with deq{$deq$}d\_\textbraceleft eq\textbraceright{} regardless of shape. Simultaneous measurements of orientation and rotation for {$\lambda$}=4{$\lambda$}=4\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D706\textbraceright =4 particles allows a decomposition of rotation along the primary axes of each particle. This analysis shows that there is no preference for rotation about a particle's symmetry axis, unlike the preference displayed by sub-Kolmogorov-scale particles in previous studies.},
