opies with intermediate density, the overlying flow perceives a deeper zero-plane displacement into the canopy, which is consistent with observations reported by previous studies, but exhibits a lower K\'arm\'an constant, {$K\approx$}0.34--0.36{$\kappa\approx$}0.34--0.36\textbackslash kappa \textbackslash approx 0.34\textbackslash unicode\textbraceleft x2013\textbraceright 0.36. For sparse canopies, {$K\kappa\backslash$}kappa tends back to its smooth-wall value, and the zero-plane displacement height is at the canopy bed. For all canopies studied, the decrease in {$K\kappa\backslash$}kappa never exceeds 15\%15\%15\textbackslash,\textbackslash\%, which is significantly less than that obtained in some previous works using conventional methods to assess outer-layer similarity.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {turbulent boundary layers},
  file = {/home/mq/Zotero/storage/39SXNYU4/Chen and García-Mayoral - 2023 - Examination of outer-layer similarity in wall turb.pdf}
}

