  doi = {10.1063/1.2844875},
  urldate = {2022-08-17},
  abstract = {Flow past a circular cylinder of finite span-length with two free ends is investigated based on direct numerical solutions of the three-dimensional unsteady incompressible Navier--Stokes equations. Special attention is paid to the effect of length-to-diameter ratio  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  on vortex shedding from the cylinder into its wake, where  {$L$} L  is the span-length and  {$D$} D  is the diameter of the cylinder. The length-to-diameter ratio  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  is prescribed in the range of  0.5{$\leqL$}/{$D\leq$}100 0.5{$\leq$}L/D{$\leq$}100 , and the Reynolds number Re which is based on  {$D$} D  is  40{$\leq$}Re{$\leq$}300 40{$\leq$}Re{$\leq$}300 . Results show that vortex shedding from, and thus wake pattern behind, the cylinder changes drastically depending on both  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  and Re. Five basic patterns of vortex shedding are found to exist: (i) Periodic oblique vortex shedding at relatively large  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  and at Re beyond a critical Reynolds number, (ii) quasiperiodic oblique vortex shedding also at relatively large  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  but at Re below the critical Reynolds number, (iii) periodic shedding of hairpin-shaped vortices that occurs when  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  is moderate, (iv) steady two counter-rotating vortex pairs that appear when both  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  and Re are small, and (v) alternate shedding of a counter-rotating vortex pair from two flat ends, which occurs when  {$L$}/{$D$} L/D  is small but Re is high. Transition processes between the basic patterns are also examined. Three-dimensional vortical structures in the wake are clarified in some detail.},
