  urldate = {2025-02-16},
  abstract = {, Direct numerical simulations have been performed for turbulent thermal convection between horizontal no-slip, permeable walls with a distance HH and a constant temperature difference {$\Delta$}T\textbackslash Delta T at the Rayleigh number Ra=3\texttimes 103--1010Ra=3\textbackslash times 10\textasciicircum 3\textbackslash text \textbraceleft --\textbraceright 10\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 10\textbraceright. On the no-slip wall surfaces z=0z=0, HH, the wall-normal (vertical) transpiration velocity is assumed to be proportional to the local pressure fluctuation, i.e. w=-{$\beta$}p{$\prime$}/{$\rho$}w=-\textbackslash beta p'/\textbackslash rho, +{$\beta$}p{$\prime$}/{$\rho$}+\textbackslash beta p'/\textbackslash rho (Jim\'enez et al., J. Fluid Mech., vol. 442, 2001, pp. 89--117). Here {$\rho\backslash$}rho is mass density, and the property of the permeable wall is given by the permeability parameter {$\beta$}U\textbackslash beta U normalised with the buoyancy-induced terminal velocity U=(g{$\alpha\Delta$}TH)1/2U=(g\textbackslash alpha \textbackslash Delta TH)\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 1/2\textbraceright, where gg and {$\alpha\backslash$}alpha are acceleration due to gravity and volumetric thermal expansivity, respectively. The critical transition of heat transfer in convective turbulence has been found between the two RaRa regimes for fixed {$\beta$}U=3\textbackslash beta U=3 and fixed Prandtl number Pr=1Pr=1. In the subcritical regime at lower RaRa the Nusselt number NuNu scales with RaRa as Nu{$\sim$}Ra1/3Nu\textbackslash sim Ra\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 1/3\textbraceright, as commonly observed in turbulent Rayleigh--B\'enard convection. In the supercritical regime at higher RaRa, on the other hand, the ultimate scaling Nu{$\sim$}Ra1/2Nu\textbackslash sim Ra\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 1/2\textbraceright{} is achieved, meaning that the wall-to-wall heat flux scales with U{$\Delta$}TU\textbackslash Delta T independent of the thermal diffusivity, although the heat transfer on the wall is dominated by thermal conduction. In the supercritical permeable case, large-scale motion is induced by buoyancy even in the vicinity of the wall, leading to significant transpiration velocity of the order of UU. The ultimate heat transfer is attributed to this large-scale significant fluid motion rather than to transition to turbulence in boundary-layer flow. In such `wall-bounded' convective turbulence, a thermal conduction layer still exists on the wall, but there is no near-wall layer of large change in the vertical velocity, suggesting that the effect of the viscosity is negligible even in the near-wall region. The balance between the dominant advection and buoyancy terms in the vertical Boussinesq equation gives us the velocity scale of O(U)O(U) in the whole region, so that the total energy budget equation implies the Taylor dissipation law {$\epsilon\sim$}U3/H\textbackslash epsilon \textbackslash sim U\textasciicircum 3/H and the ultimate scaling Nu{$\sim$}Ra1/2Nu\textbackslash sim Ra\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 1/2\textbraceright.},
