21, 2018, 024502), and suggest a universal linear mechanism underlying the onset of turbulence in both channel and pipe flows of sufficiently elastic dilute polymer solutions. Although the centre-mode instability continues down to {$B\sim$}10-2{$\beta\sim$}10-2\textbackslash beta \textbackslash sim 10\textasciicircum\textbraceleft -2\textbraceright{} for pipe flow, it ceases to exist for {$B<$}0.5{$\beta<$}0.5\textbackslash beta {$<$} 0.5 in channels. Whereas inertia, elasticity and solvent viscous effects are simultaneously required for this instability, a higher viscous threshold is required for channel flow. Further, in the opposite limit of {$B\rightarrow$}1{$\beta\rightarrow$}1\textbackslash beta \textbackslash rightarrow 1, the centre-mode instability in channel flow continues to exist at {$Re\approx$}5Re{$\approx$}5Re \textbackslash approx 5, again in contrast to pipe flow where the instability ceases to exist below {$Re\approx$}63Re{$\approx$}63Re \textbackslash approx 63, regardless of {$E$}EE or {$B\beta\backslash$}beta. Our predictions are in reasonable agreement with experimental observations for the onset of turbulence in the flow of polymer solutions through microchannels.},
