aceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft +\textbraceright, appears to tend towards a constant value, {$\Delta\Theta$}+{$FR\Delta\Theta$}FR+\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x0394\textbraceright\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D6E9\textbraceright\_\textbraceleft FR\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft +\textbraceright. This corresponds to the Stanton number (the temperature analogue of the skin-friction coefficient) monotonically decreasing with {$Reb$}RebRe\_\textbraceleft b\textbraceright{} in the fully rough regime. Using shifted logarithmic velocity and temperature profiles, the heat-transfer law as described by the Stanton number in the fully rough regime can be derived once both the equivalent sand-grain roughness {$ks$}/{$k$}ks/kk\_\textbraceleft s\textbraceright/k and the temperature difference {$\Delta\Theta$}+{$FR\Delta\Theta$}FR+\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x0394\textbraceright\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D6E9\textbraceright\_\textbraceleft FR\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft +\textbraceright{} are known. In meteorology, this corresponds to the ratio of momentum and heat-transfer roughness lengths, {$z$}0{$m$}/{$z$}0ℎz0m/z0hz\_\textbraceleft 0m\textbraceright/z\_\textbraceleft 0h\textbraceright, being linearly proportional to the inner-normalised momentum roughness length, {$z$}+0{$m$}z0m+z\_\textbraceleft 0m\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft +\textbraceright, where the constant of proportionality is related to {$\Delta\Theta$}+{$FR\Delta\Theta$}FR+\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x0394\textbraceright\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D6E9\textbraceright\_\textbraceleft FR\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft +\textbraceright. While Reynolds analogy, or similarity between momentum and heat transfer, breaks down for the bulk skin-friction and heat-transfer coefficients, similar distribution patterns between the heat flux and viscous component of the wall shear stress are observed. Instantaneous visualisations of the temperature field show a thin thermal diffusive sublayer following the roughness geometry in the fully rough regime, resembling the viscous sublayer of a contorted smooth wall.},
  langid = {english},
