  abstract = {An active oscillation control method is applied to the walls of compressible sub- and supersonic turbulent channel flow with the aim of friction drag reduction. Combinations of three bulk Mach numbers Mab=[0.3,1.5,3.0] and semi-local Reynolds numbers matching at the channel center Re{$\tau$}c{$\ast$}=[150,400,800] are first used to simulate unaffected and controlled flows. Consistent with increased length scales, larger normal stress anisotropies and coherence of vortical structures for the higher Mach number uncontrolled flows, a need for larger optimum control wavelengths and improved efficiency is observed for the controlled cases compared to the corresponding incompressible flows. This is accompanied by a more effective attenuation of the pressure-strain correlation and dissipation in the wall-parallel directions for the controlled flows with increasing Mach number. A mitigation of the near-wall compressibility effects occurs with increasing Reynolds number at fixed bulk Mach number, which attenuates the drag reduction benefits. Following recent approaches in the literature (e.g. (Gattere et al., 2024; Hasan et al., 2024)), cooling terms were introduced in the Navier--Stokes equations to minimize variable property effects within the near-wall region or the entire channel, respectively. The influence of variable property and intrinsic compressibility effects on the observed behavior and control effects could be investigated using this approach. Intrinsic compressibility effects are found to contribute significantly to the change in Reynolds stresses and pressure-strain correlations. They also contribute significantly to the change in control wavelength and drag reduction efficiency with increasing Mach number.},
