  abstract = {Measurements of concentrations, translational velocities, orientations and rotation rates of seven, straight and rigid fiber types in a fully developed turbulent water channel flow (one-way coupled, Re{$\tau$}=435) are reported. The aim was to study inertial and length effects on fiber kinematics. Fibers were characterized by Stokes numbers and mean, normalized lengths ranging between 0.02 \textexclamdown{} St \textexclamdown{} 0.34 and 16 {$\leq$}L\textasciimacron +{$\leq$} 66.8. Except for the longest (L\textasciimacron + = 66.8) fibers, a depletion layer was observed near the wall, its width increasing with decreasing St (for a given length). Away from the wall, fibers lagged the fluid, increasingly so with increasing St and L\textasciimacron + due to increased drag. In the buffer layer, fiber excess velocities decreased with L\textasciimacron + while inertial effects were small. Irrespective of fiber length, all fibers strongly aligned with the streamwise direction in the near-wall region. Fiber rotation rates peak in the buffer layer, and remain nearly constant in the log-layer and wake region. However, peak values decreased with increasing L\textasciimacron +. Our results suggest intricate coupling between fiber inertia and length in conjunction with the governing turbulence scales.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {Anisotropic particles,Digital holography,Fibers,Orientation and rotation rates,Wall-bounded turbulence},
