  abstract = {, The characteristics of turbulent boundary layers (TBLs) subjected to adverse pressure gradients are analysed through well-resolved large-eddy simulations. The geometries under study are the NACA0012 and NACA4412 wing sections, at                                                 0{$\circ$}0\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash circ \textbraceright{}                            and                                                 5{$\circ$}5\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash circ \textbraceright{}                            angle of attack, respectively, both of them at a Reynolds number based on inflow velocity and chord length of                                                 Rec=400000Re\_\textbraceleft c\textbraceright =400\textbackslash,000                           . The turbulence statistics show that adverse pressure gradients (APGs) have a significant effect on the mean velocity, velocity fluctuations and turbulent kinetic energy budget, and this effect is more prominent on the outer region of the boundary layer. Furthermore, the effect of flow history is assessed by means of an integrated Clauser pressure-gradient parameter                                                 \textasciimacron{$B\backslash$}overline\textbraceleft\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D6FD\textbraceright\textbraceright{}                            (Vinuesa et~al., Flow Turbul. Combust., vol.~99, 2017, pp. 565--587), through the study of cases with matching local values of                                                 {$B\backslash$}unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D6FD\textbraceright{}                            and the friction Reynolds number                                                 Re{$T$}Re\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash unicode[STIX]\textbraceleft x1D70F\textbraceright\textbraceright{}                            to isolate this effect. Our results show a noticeable effect of the flow history on the outer region, however the differences in the near-wall peak of the tangential velocity fluctuations appear to be mostly produced by the local APG magnitude. The one-dimensional power-spectral density shows energetic small scales in the outer region of APG TBLs, whereas these energetic scales do not appear in zero-pressure-gradient (ZPG) TBLs, suggesting that small scales near the wall are advected towards the outer layer by the APG. Moreover, the linear coherence spectra show that the spectral outer peak of high-Reynolds-number ZPG TBLs is highly correlated with the near-wall region (Baars et~al., J.~Fluid Mech., vol.~823, 2017, R2), unlike APG TBLs which do not show such a correlation. This result, together with the different two-dimensional spectra of APG and high-Reynolds-number ZPG TBLs, suggests different energisation mechanisms due to APG and increase in Reynolds number. To the authors' knowledge, this is the first in-depth analysis of the TBL characteristics over wings, including detailed single-point statistics, spectra and coherence.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {turbulence simulation},
  file = {/home/mq/Zotero/storage/6IAFASKE/Tanarro et al. - 2020 - Effect of adverse pressure gradients on turbulent .pdf}
}

