  abstract = {Large eddy simulations of turbulent flow over a sphere are conducted at subcritical Reynolds numbers ( Re=3700 Re=3700  and  10 4 104 ) based on the freestream velocity and sphere diameter. At  Re=3700 Re=3700 , the separating shear layer persists downstream to form a cylindrical vortex sheet and its instability becomes manifest at  x{$\approx$}2d x{$\approx$}2d . The flow right behind the sphere contains only a few vortices. On the other hand, at  Re= 10 4 Re=104 , the shear-layer instability occurs right behind the sphere in a form of vortex rings, and the flow becomes turbulent in the near wake. Therefore, at  Re= 10 4 Re=104 , the size of the recirculation region is smaller and the wake recovers more quickly than at  Re=3700 Re=3700 . At both Reynolds numbers, large-scale waviness of vortical structures is observed in the wake and the plane containing the large-scale waviness changes quasirandomly in time. This waviness is more pronounced at  Re= 10 4 Re=104  than at  Re=3700 Re=3700 . The mechanism responsible for this large-scale waviness of vortical structures is shown to be closely associated with the temporal evolution of vortices generated by the shear-layer instability.},
